{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.index.as-observer.md | sed -n '124,190p;493,589p;739,783p'; git diff --check HEAD~1 HEAD -- docs/design/dao.space.index.as-observer.md\"","aggregated_output":"   124\t```\n   125\tindex-state = {:indexes  {:eavt bt :aevt bt :avet bt :vaet bt}   ; dao.data.btree values, structurally shared across batches\n   126\t               :storage  <IStorage over a content handle>       ; the trees' storage; persists across publishes (§4.1)\n   127\t               :mode     :resolved | :unresolved                 ; fixed at construction (§3.1)\n   128\t               :ids      nil | {:next-eid n}                     ; allocator; present only in :unresolved mode\n   129\t               :batch    n                                       ; ordinal of the next batch, observer-local\n   130\t               :schema   {attr {:db/valueType :db.type/ref ...}} ; supplied; which attrs are refs\n   131\t               :max-t    nil | n                                 ; greatest writer t folded; nil until one is (§4.2)\n   132\t               :rejected n                                       ; monotonic count of rejected batches — never drained (§2.2)\n   133\t               :publish  nil | {:intake writer\n   134\t                                :staged nil | {:payloads [...] :next i}}   ; optional, §4.1\n   135\t               :defects  []}                                     ; drainable diagnostics: one event per rejected batch since last drain\n   136\t\n   137\t;; all in dao.space.index, beside index-datoms and publish-index!\n   138\t;; over the consumer alone:\n   139\tready?           (fn [x] (nil? (get-in x [:publish :staged])))\n   140\tload             index/fold-batch                              ; pure; §2.2\n   141\trun              index/flush-staged                            ; resumes a staged publication at :next; identity otherwise (§4.1)\n   142\tindex/publish!   index-state → index-state'                    ; explicit: stage this state's trees, then flush (§4.1)\n   143\tindex/drain      index-state → [index-state' defects]          ; clears :defects only; :rejected is untouched\n   144\tindex/db-value   index-state → query value                     ; §5: the covered trees as a dao.space.query source\n   145\t;; over the whole {:observer :consumer} session — the cursor and gap count live in the observer half:\n   146\tindex/coverage   session → {:cursor c :ingress-gaps g :rejected n :batch b :max-t t}   ; §5\n   147\tindex/checkpoint session → candidate                           ; §4.2: publish!'s coverage, captured at its boundary\n   148\tindex/restore    candidate storage-opts → index-state          ; §4.2: the consumer half; the composition re-attaches the observer at :cursor\n   149\t```\n   150\t\n   151\t`coverage` and `checkpoint` are the two operations that read both halves of a\n   152\tsession. They are index-side functions over a shape `dao.stream.observer`\n   153\tpublishes (`{:stream :cursor :ingress-gaps}`) — the dependency runs\n   154\t`dao.space.index → dao.stream.observer`, never the reverse — and the\n   155\tgeneric loop stays ignorant of every index field.\n   156\t\n   157\tDriven as `(run-on-stream {:observer o :consumer index-state} ready?\n   158\tindex/fold-batch index/flush-staged)` over any `dao.stream` reader\n   159\thandle attached through `dao.stream.observer/attach`. The coordination\n   160\tinspects no field of the index state; it drives it as readily as a VM —\n   161\twhich is the point of decision 2. **Publication is an explicit composition\n   162\tstep**, not a policy inside the loop: the composition calls `publish!`\n   163\tbetween rounds (after every *n* rounds, on `blocked`, on a timer — its\n   164\tchoice), and only the *resumption* of a staged publication lives inside\n   165\t`run`, so that `ready?` stays false until the intake has accepted every\n   166\tpayload. Calling `publish!` while a publication is still staged is a caller\n   167\terror (the composition checks `ready?` first); a staged publication is never\n   168\treplaced, only resumed (§4.1). `:ids` records whether it is `:owned` by this\n   169\tsession or `:shared` with others (§3.1); the distinction governs what a\n   170\tcheckpoint may restore (§4.2).\n   171\t\n   172\t### 2.2 `fold-batch`\n   173\t\n   174\tOne batch, one pure fold. **The fold is atomic per batch**: steps 0–2\n   175\tvalidate and plan against the *whole* normalized batch before step 3\n   176\ttouches a tree or step 4 advances `:ids`; a batch rejected at any step\n   177\tleaves all four trees, `:ids`, and `:max-t` unchanged, increments the\n   178\tmonotonic `:rejected` count, appends exactly one drainable defect event\n   179\t(batch ordinal, position, reason), and advances `:batch` by exactly one —\n   180\tnever zero, or every later cross-session ordinal (§3.2) would drift on the\n   181\tfirst malformed input even without a `gap`. `:rejected` is the durable\n   182\trecord that the index is partial; `:defects` is the diagnostic a driver\n   183\treads and clears. Draining never touches `:rejected`, so completeness\n   184\tsurvives a drain, a checkpoint, and a restore; a composition that needs the\n   185\t*identities* of omitted batches retains the drained events itself. An\n   186\t**empty** admitted batch is a valid no-op: it advances `:batch` once and\n   187\tchanges nothing else — not trees, `:ids`, `:max-t`, or `:rejected`.\n   188\t\n   189\t0. **Normalize the outer value.** `run-on-stream` hands `load` one stream\n   190\t   value, and media differ in what that is: a transactor appends one\n   493\tcheckpoint here is a *value with a validity contract*, in three parts.\n   494\t\n   495\t**Capture at one boundary.** `publish!` is called between rounds, when the\n   496\tsession's cursor sits exactly after the last batch its trees cover. It\n   497\tstages the publication on the consumer; `checkpoint`, called on the\n   498\t**session** at that same boundary — before any further round — captures a\n   499\t**candidate**\n   500\t\n   501\t```\n   502\t{:manifest-address a  :cursor c  :ids i  :batch n  :max-t t\n   503\t :ingress-gaps g  :rejected r  :mode m  :schema-hash h}\n   504\t```\n   505\t\n   506\tfrom both halves: the manifest that will name the trees, the observer's\n   507\tcursor after the last folded batch and its gap count, and the consumer's\n   508\tallocator, ordinal, greatest writer `t` (§2.2 step 3), monotonic rejected\n   509\tcount, mode, and schema. The cursor and gap count are not in the index\n   510\tstate — `run-on-stream` advances them in the observer half, including on a\n   511\t`gap` — which is why capture takes the session; the rule that nothing is\n   512\tfolded between `publish!` and `checkpoint` binds whoever calls the two, and\n   513\tthe two are meant to be adjacent calls in the composition. A candidate\n   514\twhose cursor were later than its manifest's coverage would, on restart,\n   515\tskip every batch in between permanently.\n   516\t\n   517\t**A candidate becomes a checkpoint only when it is recoverable.** Intake\n   518\tsuccess means enqueued, not materialized (`dao.space.index.md`); a process\n   519\tcan fail with the manifest, or blobs it names, still in an in-memory intake\n   520\t— exactly the loss window `dao.space.md` §Fault Tolerance documents. A\n   521\tcandidate is promoted to a checkpoint by the composition, and only after it\n   522\thas verified, **through the durable store's read path** (`jing/get`, never\n   523\tthe recording handle or any cache whose presence proves nothing about\n   524\tdurability), that the manifest reads back valid (`read-manifest`) and that\n   525\tevery address reachable from **all four roots** resolves — checked inside\n   526\tthe `bt/walk-addresses` visitor, leaves included, because the walker does\n   527\tnot itself restore leaves. Missing content or a read failure is\n   528\t\"verification incomplete\", never success; the check may be retried later,\n   529\tand because content is immutable and retained, a check that once passed\n   530\tstays passed. No acknowledgement from the asynchronous DaoJing observer is\n   531\tneeded for a synchronously readable store; an async-only store needs an\n   532\tasynchronous verification mechanism, which is a capability prerequisite,\n   533\tnot a change to this rule. Until promotion the previous checkpoint stands.\n   534\tRecovery from a latest publication that never became recoverable is\n   535\ttherefore never a special case: the composition resumes from the last\n   536\t*verified* checkpoint and re-folds the suffix. Promotion proves the\n   537\tsnapshot is reopenable; that the checkpoint *record* itself is recoverable,\n   538\tand that the suffix after `c` is still retained on the medium, are the\n   539\tcomposition's separate obligations.\n   540\t\n   541\t**Resumption is bound.** `restore` refuses a checkpoint whose `:mode` or\n   542\t`:schema-hash` differ from the session being constructed, **and refuses one\n   543\twhose `:ids` was `:shared`**: a session-local snapshot of a shared allocator\n   544\tis stale the moment any sibling allocates after it, and restoring it would\n   545\tre-mint ids siblings already hold (session A checkpoints at 20, B allocates\n   546\t20–29, A restores and allocates 20 again). Recovery of a shared allocation\n   547\tdomain is a composition-level checkpoint over *every* session that shares\n   548\tit, and is deferred (§8); this note's checkpoint/restore contract covers\n   549\tindependently allocated sessions only. For those, `restore` rebuilds the\n   550\tfour trees lazily through a session-constructed `:strong` `kv-storage` over\n   551\tthe durable store (§4.1 — not the query read path) and reinstates `:ids`,\n   552\t`:batch`, `:max-t`, and `:rejected` on the consumer; the composition\n   553\tre-attaches the observer at `c` with `:ingress-gaps` reinstated (a partial\n   554\tindex stays partial after restart; a fresh zero would make §3.2's offset\n   555\tcheck look safe again). Re-attaching *at a cursor* is a small addition to\n   556\t`dao.stream.observer/attach`, which mints at `:oldest` today: it accepts\n   557\ta kept cursor, which `dao.stream.md` already says \"covers repositioning\",\n   558\tand the transport validates it on the first `next`. Then folding continues;\n   559\tnothing is replayed from the origin.\n   560\t\n   561\t**The watermark.** The transactor's `create!` needs `0` for an empty\n   562\thistory, else one plus the greatest `t` retained. A checkpoint covering\n   563\t`t ≤ 10` cannot answer that alone if transactions through `t = 12` exist\n   564\tafter its cursor. So the answer is `:max-t` — a writer-time summary derived\n   565\tfrom the folded rows at capture, causally bound to `:cursor`, never derived\n   566\tfrom observer ordinals — *plus the fold of the retained suffix after `c`*\n   567\tbefore writes are enabled; the watermark is then `0` if `:max-t` is still\n   568\t`nil` (nothing folded, the transactor's empty case) and `max-t + 1`\n   569\totherwise, and an empty checkpoint reopened in-process yields `0`, not `1`.\n   570\tThree cases, distinct:\n   571\t\n   572\t- **Same logical stream, reopened in-process** (the writer-task restart\n   573\t  `dao.space.md` §Fault Tolerance names): restore the checkpoint, fold the\n   574\t  suffix from `c` to `blocked`, then derive as above. This is the O(suffix)\n   575\t  relief the transactor asked for.\n   576\t- **Process restart over a durable medium** (`dao.stream.file.md`, cursors\n   577\t  survive): the same, over the reopened medium.\n   578\t- **Process restart over a memory-log**: a new, empty logical stream with a\n   579\t  new identity; the checkpoint's cursor names nothing on it, the session\n   580\t  starts at `:oldest`, and the watermark is the transactor's today (`0` for\n   581\t  empty history).\n   582\t\n   583\t\"One truth\" is honoured rather than assumed: `:max-t` is not a second\n   584\tcounter that can disagree with the log; it is the log's own `t`, summarized\n   585\tat a named cursor and only ever advanced by folding the log. Where the\n   586\tcheckpoint *record* lives — on the observed medium or beside the manifest\n   587\tin `dao.jing` — remains open (§8); its validity conditions above do not.\n   588\t\n   589\t---\n   739\tan initial expansion's external coordinate joins to the source entity\n   740\tthrough resolution facts by value; a nested expansion's log-local ref\n   741\tresolves within the log batch; the same negative number on two media names\n   742\ttwo entities; a `v`-only and an `m`-only tempid each get an id and facts;\n   743\tthe `yin.vm.macro.md` §4.1 chain source-call → event → expansion-root\n   744\tanswered by `dao.space.query/q` over the three indexed media; after a\n   745\tforced `gap` on one session the offset is reported void.\n   746\t\n   747\t**Phase 3 — checkpoint validity and recovery.** Over a file-backed medium\n   748\t(`dao.stream.file`): capture a candidate, verify it against the durable\n   749\tstore, restart from it, fold the suffix, and assert the resumed index\n   750\tequals a from-origin index and the watermark equals `max-t + 1` over\n   751\tcheckpoint plus suffix; a candidate whose manifest was never materialized is\n   752\tnot promoted and restart uses the previous checkpoint; restored\n   753\t`:ingress-gaps`/`:defects` survive; a same-logical-stream in-process reopen\n   754\trestores, folds the suffix, and only then enables writes. Over a\n   755\tmemory-log, assert the documented behaviour instead: a resumed session\n   756\tstarts fresh at `:oldest` of the new log and the checkpoint is not\n   757\tconsulted. The transactor's `create!` derives its watermark from a\n   758\tcheckpoint plus suffix when one is offered.\n   759\t\n   760\t---\n   761\t\n   762\t## 8. Open questions\n   763\t\n   764\t- **One session per medium, or one over a merged view?** Per medium keeps\n   765\t  each cursor honest and makes `gap` attributable; a merged view needs a\n   766\t  merge interpreter that `dao.stream.md` does not (and should not) provide.\n   767\t  This note leans per-medium with a shared allocator, but the allocator\n   768\t  then couples the sessions — a composition choice to state when it is\n   769\t  made.\n   770\t- **Retention and `gap`.** Over an evicting transport the observer inherits\n   771\t  `dao.stream.observer`'s gap accounting; an index with a gap is a partial\n   772\t  index and must say so, and a cross-session ordinal offset (§3.2) is void\n   773\t  after one. Whether a published manifest should carry the gap count is\n   774\t  undecided.\n   775\t- **Where the checkpoint lives** (§4.2).\n   776\t- **Recovery of a shared allocation domain.** `restore` refuses a\n   777\t  `:shared` `:ids` (§4.2); a composition that shares an allocator across\n   778\t  `:unresolved` sessions needs a checkpoint over all of them at once and a\n   779\t  replay discipline that reassigns the same ids in the same order. Deferred\n   780\t  with Phase 2's topology choice.\n   781\t- **Whether `snapshot` is retired** in favour of \"run a throwaway session to\n   782\t  `blocked` and take its trees\". Probably, but `snapshot`'s status\n   783\t  vocabulary (`:ended`/`:blocked`/`:gap`/`:defect`) should survive as the\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-13 09:32:43 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-13 16:32:43 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\nCoding-Agent: codex\nSession-ID: 01a099f1-87d6-7811-8a69-b3336b956fac\nRole: Lead System Architect (review) | Model: gpt-6-astra\n\nReviewed HEAD `a4395d8`. Read-only; no files written. All locations below refer to `docs/design/dao.space.index.as-observer.md`.\n\n| Finding | Status | Resolution |\n|---|---|---|\n| R1 — access to observer state | **Resolved** | §2.1:145–155 gives `coverage` and `checkpoint` the whole session. §4.2:495–515 binds capture to the publication boundary; 541–559 separates consumer restoration from observer reattachment. |\n| R2 — completeness survives draining | **Resolved** | §2.2:174–187 separates monotonic `:rejected` from drainable diagnostics. The candidate carries `:rejected`, and restoration reinstates it. |\n| R3 — reserved-id partition and parity | **Resolved** | §3.1:308–326 defines disjoint categories and admits reserved `e` in resolved mode, preserving existing behavior for that case. |\n| R4 — empty batches and watermark | **Resolved** | §2.2 defines empty batches as ordinal-only progress, initializes `:max-t` to `nil`, and specifies within-row allocation order. §4.2:561–569 derives watermark zero for empty history. |\n| R5 — shared allocator rewind | **Resolved by explicit scope restriction** | §4.2:541–549 rejects shared-allocator restoration. §8:776–780 defers coordinated recovery explicitly; the sibling-allocation rejection test is specified. |\n\n**[P3 — suggestion/alignment] Phase 3 retains two stale assertions.**\n\nLocation: `docs/design/dao.space.index.as-observer.md:750–753`.\n\nThe test paragraph still says watermark is always `max-t + 1` and that `:defects` survives restoration. The authoritative contract now uses zero when `:max-t` is `nil` and persists `:rejected`, not drainable diagnostics.\n\n**Correction:** Update those assertions to the conditional watermark rule and `:ingress-gaps`/`:rejected`. This is test-description cleanup; the corrected contracts and Phase 0′ tests already establish the intended behavior.\n\n**Shared-domain checkpoint boundary**\n\nRefusing `:shared` in `restore` is the right mandatory safety boundary. It prevents allocation-domain corruption even if a caller supplies a previously captured record.\n\nA shared session may still publish its trees and capture a candidate for inspection or future composition-level coordination. However, that candidate should not be advertised or selected as an independently resumable checkpoint under the current contract. I recommend rejecting promotion for `:shared`, or explicitly labeling the captured value as snapshot-only until coordinated recovery exists. There is no need to forbid publication or observational capture.\n\nThe present scope restriction is sufficient for approval; coordinated recovery is intentionally deferred rather than implicitly promised.\n\n**Placement of `coverage` and `checkpoint`**\n\nI have no architectural objection to keeping them in `dao.space.index`. They consume the generic observer’s public session shape in the permitted dependency direction. They neither expose index semantics to the observer loop nor introduce knowledge of sibling evaluators.\n\nReading an opaque cursor and gap count is ordinary composition over protocol state. It does not collapse interpretation and execution. A helper outside both namespaces would be an organizational alternative, not an invariant improvement. Keep these functions data-only: no cursor inspection, sibling discovery or hidden stream operations.\n\nThe adjacent publication/capture calls are also acceptable as an explicit composition contract, provided the caller installs the returned consumer before capture and runs no intervening observer round.\n\nThe strengthened promotion rule correctly checks the manifest and every reachable address across all four roots through the durable read path, including leaves. Checkpoint-record availability and retained-suffix availability are now correctly separate obligations.\n\nNo blocking architectural findings remain. Approval covers the design with its stated implementation prerequisites, including partial-session exception handling, kept-cursor attachment, and the macro provenance amendment.\n\n**Verdict: APPROVE.**"}}
